sed one of each. So in my hand, the W9 is an extra. That makes sense. So my hand has W2, W6, W9, T1, T7, T8, T9, J3. The melds are two chows: W7-8-9 and T1-2-3. So the T1 in my hand is part of the chi, but I still have it in my hand? That might not be possible. Wait, maybe the T1 in my hand is the one I used in the chi, but I still hold it? No, in Mahjong, once you form a meld, you place it in front of you, so you don't hold it in your hand anymore. So the T1 in my hand must be a separate tile, not part of the chi. Therefore, the chi [T1, T2, T3] must have been formed by taking T2 and T3 from my hand and adding the T1 from the previous player's discard. Wait, the game history includes \"Chi T2\", which according to the note means that the player formed a chi for the sequence [T1, T2, T3], where the T2 was the discarded tile. So the player already had T1 and T3 in hand, and took T2 from the previous player. Therefore, in my hand, I still have the T1 and T3? No, because once you form a chi, you place the tiles in the meld. So the T1 in my hand is part of the chi, but I still have it in my hand? That doesn't make sense. Maybe the T1 in my hand is a duplicate. Wait, the meld [T1, T2, T3] is a chi, which requires three tiles. So if I had two T1s and took T2 from the discard, then I would have a chi of T1, T2, T3. But in my hand, I have T1, T7, T8, T9. So maybe the T1 in my hand is part of the chi, but I still hold it. That's possible if I formed the chi by taking the T2. So the T1 is part of the chi but remains in my hand. Hmm, maybe I need to accept that and focus on the current hand.\n\nNow, my goal is to evaluate which tile to discard next. The valid actions are Play T9, W6, T8, W9, T7, T1, W2, J3.\n\nLooking at my hand, I have 8 tiles: W2, W6, W9, T1, T7, T8, T9, J3. The melds are two chows. So my hand is these 8 tiles. I need to discard one to keep 7 tiles, and eventually aim to win.\n\nFirst, let's see what kind of hand I have. The melds are two chows: W7-8-9 and T